# Test temporary-file ownership

## `tempnam()` creates a file, it does not just reserve a name

This is the rule the whole file exists for. `tempnam()` returns a path **and
creates the file at it**. Any code that derives a second path from the returned
one therefore owns **two** filesystem artifacts:

```php
// WRONG — owns two paths, cleans one.
$path = tempnam(sys_get_temp_dir(), 'thing-').'.pdf';
// tempnam created /tmp/thing-ABC123     <- leaked, zero bytes, forever
// the write lands on /tmp/thing-ABC123.pdf  <- the only one unlink() sees
```

Prefer removing the second artifact over adding a second `unlink`. If the tool
you are feeding does not care about the extension, **use the allocation itself
as the path** — then there is no path left to forget:

```php
// RIGHT — one owned path.
$path = tempnam(sys_get_temp_dir(), 'thing-');
file_put_contents($path, $bytes);

try {
    return $callback($path);
} finally {
    @unlink($path);
}
```

Poppler (`pdftotext`, `pdfinfo`) dispatches on the file header, not the
filename — a `.pdf` suffix buys nothing there. Verify the requirement for your
own tool before keeping a suffix; do not carry one out of habit.

If a suffix genuinely *is* required, the derived path must be accounted for
**and** the original allocation must be explicitly removed or reused. Both
paths are yours.

## Every temporary artifact has one explicit owner

A test-created temporary file must have a single owner and deterministic
cleanup. Make it obvious from the code which paths are created, which one
carries the payload, and which are removed.

## Cleanup runs on the failure path too

Use `finally`. Cleanup must execute when the callback or the rendering
operation throws — **and must preserve the original failure**. Never swallow an
exception in order to clean up; the primary failure is the more valuable signal.

## Leak tests compare the owned set, not the directory

Assert on the artifacts *this helper* created, before versus after. A global
count of the temporary directory is **not authoritative**: other processes on
the machine may add or remove files in the same window, so the count moves for
reasons unrelated to the code under test.

Strongest available assertion: from inside the callback, verify that exactly one
artifact is live and that it **is** the path handed out. That catches the
derive-and-forget shape directly rather than inferring it from a count.

## Never glob-delete the temporary directory

```
rm -f /tmp/thing-*
```

is not cleanup. It hides continued leakage, and it deletes pre-existing files
that belong to other processes, other sprints, or standing evidence. Clean the
artifacts you created. Measure pre-existing ones; do not delete them to
manufacture a zero count.

## Keep temporary allocation atomic and opaque

Do not replace `tempnam()` with `uniqid()`, a timestamp, or a fixed filename to
avoid cleanup complexity — that discards atomic collision-safe allocation and
introduces races between concurrent test processes. Never weaken permissions
(`chmod 0777`). Never build a temporary filename from user- or record-derived
input such as a patient name or medical-record number; temporary names stay
opaque.

## Two lifecycles, two owners

`finally` is the whole contract only when the artifact is created, used and
destroyed inside one call. `pdfWithTempFile` is that pattern.

An artifact that must **outlive** its creator has no `finally` that can reach
it — a stub PATH directory a child process still has to read, an `UploadedFile`
whose HTTP request has not run yet. Those go through `tempArtifactFile()` /
`tempArtifactDir()` in `tests/Pest.php`; the registry behind them is the owner,
and the global `afterEach` drains it on the failing path as well as the passing
one. That is where the pre-existing hand-written cleanup calls were being
skipped.

Registry cleanup removes **exactly the recorded paths**. It is never a prefix
glob: a concurrent test process's artifacts and the historical orphans of
earlier runs must be safe by construction, not by luck.

## The remover never follows a link, and never leaves /tmp

A link to a *file* is not the dangerous case — `unlink()` never follows a
symlink, so even an unguarded remover destroys only the link. The catastrophic
case is a link to a *directory*: `is_dir()` follows it, so a remover without an
`is_link()` test descends INTO the target and deletes its contents. Fixtures
deliberately contain such links (`ci-bare-host-` symlinks `/bin/bash` onto a
stub PATH). Test the dangerous shape, not the benign one.

Confinement to `sys_get_temp_dir()` is a property of the code, not of every
future caller's discipline.

## A guard whose failure mode is damage is not a guard

Both rules above were found by mutation, not by review. Removing the symlink
guard left the file-link test passing, and the confinement test proved its point
by deleting `composer.json`. Point destructive assertions at a fixture you
created.

## Anti-regression

Pinned behaviourally by `tests/Feature/Cicd/PdfTempFileLifecycleContractTest.php`
(scoped lifecycle) and `tests/Feature/Cicd/TempFileSiblingLeakContractTest.php`
(deferred lifecycle), both declared in `config/ci_runner.php` →
`critical_gate_mandatory_suites` so their CI selection is enforced rather than
incidental. The sibling guard also asserts, by tokenising rather than grepping,
that no call site derives a second path from a `tempnam()` allocation — a
textual scan matches the guard's own prose and reports itself. `tempnam()` is
not banned; it is the correct atomic primitive. Deriving from it is the defect.

